<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luzon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luzon is the largest and most populous island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, home to the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. Before Spanish rule it held trading polities such as Tondo and Maynila, linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Brunei. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> made Manila the capital of its colony in 1571, and Luzon was the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> of 1896–1898 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luzon.jpg" alt="Luzon" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Philippines_(Luzon).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…met by slave raiding: Iranun and Balangingi Sama fleets based in the Sulu zone seized, by his estimates, thousands of captives a year from the Visayas, <b>Luzon</b>, the Moluccas, Sulawesi and the Malay world, many of whom were set to fishing and collecting marine produce or sold onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan’s position gave the Jōmon several routes of contact: through the Korean Peninsula to Kyushu and Honshu, along the island chain of <b>Luzon</b>, Taiwan and the Ryukyus to Southeast Asia, and through Hokkaido and Sakhalin to Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>A copper plate dated to the Saka year 822 recorded the cancellation of a debt and named a leader of Tundun, usually identified with Tondo on <b>Luzon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In northern <b>Luzon</b>, a 60-man Filipino rearguard under Brigadier General Gregorio del Pilar was overcome by more than 500 Americans, but delayed their advance long enough for President Emilio Aguinaldo and his troops to escape.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its location is debated: most scholars favour Mindoro, others Bay on Laguna de Bay in <b>Luzon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ma-i">Ma-i</a></li><li><blockquote>Revolutionary armies took most of <b>Luzon</b> from Spanish garrisons.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines">Revolutionary Government of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
